  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1226 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nWidespread showers are expected to be ongoing mid-morning \nWednesday with some uncertainty how far south into southern \nOklahoma this first round will extend. A second wave of activity \nwill be developing across central Oklahoma as another shortwave \nlifts ahead of the main upper low still near the four-corners \nregion by late morning. Outflow from earlier storms will help the \ncold front sag south resulting in a more east-west boundary from \ncentral into east-central Oklahoma and western Arkansas by \nafternoon. This boundary will continue to be focus for renewed \nshowers and thunderstorms through the evening as the upper low \nbegins to approach. Heating south of the boundary will raise \ninstability enough to support some strong to severe storms. Given \nthe improved wind fields along and north of the boundary there \nwill be non-zero tornado threat along with damaging winds across \nall but far southeast Oklahoma.\n\nThe multiple rounds of convective activity will result in \nwidespread 1-3\" of rainfall generally north of I-40 through \nThursday morning with localized amounts approaching 5\" in spots. \nGiven the dry conditions in place ahead of this event and low \nprobabilities of >3\" widespread rainfall opted to not issue a \nFlood Watch at this time. Again later shifts may determine where \nthe better likelihood of storm clusters with heavy rainfall \noverlap to produce worst-case amounts in excess of 5 inches \njustifying long-fuse flood headlines. \n\nThe main upper low is forecast to eject into the Plains on \nThursday dragging a strong cold front through the area. A band of \nshowers with isolated storms should accompany the front as it \nmoves into southeast Oklahoma and northwest Arkansas by evening.\nDry and cool conditions will be in place behind the front Friday\nand Saturday with daytime highs falling into the 60s. Fall-like\nlow temperatures will carry us through the weekend before\ntemperatures slowly warm into early next week.",
